# Grammar

The Language Level 0.1–0.5 grammar remains described in the implementation brief
and the parser sources. Language Level 0.6 adds the comprehension forms below,
Language Level 0.7 adds `yield_statement` and `generator_expression`, and
Language Level 0.8 adds `class_statement`. Comprehension and generator-expression
targets remain a single `NAME`. Assignment and `for` statements accept a fixed
name list (D-0038). `star_expr`, async, nested unpack, multiple inheritance,
and `yield` as an expression (including `yield from`) are not part of this grammar.

Comparison operators include membership:

```ebnf
comp_op          ::= "<" | ">" | "==" | ">=" | "<=" | "!="
                   | "in" | "not" "in" | "is" | "is" "not"
```

```ebnf
expression       ::= or_test ["if" or_test "else" expression]
or_test          ::= and_test ("or" and_test)*
assignment       ::= target_list "=" expression_list
target_list      ::= NAME ("," NAME)* [","]
                   | "(" target_list ")"
expression_list  ::= expression ("," expression)* [","]
for_statement    ::= "for" target_list "in" expression ":" suite
                     [ "else" ":" suite ]
```

Conditional expressions bind less tightly than `or`. Else-chains are
right-associative (`a if c1 else b if c2 else c`). `lambda` is not part of
this grammar, so `expression` is the top of the Pratt/`or` ladder (D-0040).

Unparenthesized tuples remain invalid in other expression positions
(`return a, b` is still a syntax error; D-0015).

```ebnf
list_display     ::= "[" [starred_list | comprehension] "]"
set_display      ::= "{" starred_list | comprehension "}"
dict_display     ::= "{" "}" | "{" (key_datum_list | dict_comprehension) "}"

comprehension    ::= expression comp_for
dict_comprehension ::= expression ":" expression comp_for

comp_for         ::= "for" NAME "in" or_test [comp_iter]
comp_iter        ::= comp_for | comp_if
comp_if          ::= "if" or_test [comp_iter]
```

`{x for x in it}` is a set comprehension; `{k: v for k in it}` is a dict
comprehension; `{}` remains an empty dict. Comprehension names bind in the
enclosing scope (D-0026). String iterables yield one-character strings.

```ebnf
yield_statement  ::= "yield" expression
generator_expression ::= "(" expression comp_for ")"
call             ::= primary "(" [argument_list | expression comp_for] ")"
```

`yield` is a statement only; it is a syntax error outside a `def` and in any
expression position (D-0045). A generator expression needs its parentheses
except as a call's only argument, where `f(x for x in it)` uses the call
parentheses. Generator-expression names do not bind in the enclosing scope,
unlike comprehension names (D-0046).
```ebnf
class_statement  ::= "class" NAME ["(" expression ")"] ":" suite
class_suite      ::= (pass_statement | simple_assign | function_def)+
```

Class statements are module-level only. A single optional base expression is
allowed (default `object`). The suite is restricted to `pass`, simple name
assignment, and `def` (D-0049). Multiple bases are a syntax error.
